FBX: never form a reference to the one-past-the-end mapping entry

Upstream status (checked 2026-09-03 against assimp master 19f7386d7 and the
pinned tag v6.0.5): UNFIXED.  Reported upstream as assimp issue #4312,
"Out of range access of std::vector FBX::MeshGeometry::m_mappings" (opened
2021-12-29, still open).  This patch is the fix that issue proposes, hardened
for the empty-vector case; it is not a backport of any upstream commit,
because none exists.  Drop this patch the day upstream lands its own.

The bug
-------
MeshGeometry builds an adjacency table from FBX input vertices to output
vertices: m_mapping_counts[i] entries starting at m_mapping_offsets[i] inside
m_mappings.  A vertex that no polygon references gets count 0, and because the
offsets are a running sum, a trailing run of such vertices gets an offset equal
to m_mappings.size().  ToOutputVertexIndex then evaluated

    &m_mappings[ m_mapping_offsets[ in_index ] ]

which binds a reference to the one-past-the-end element.  That is undefined
behaviour even though the caller never dereferences it (it loops [0, count)),
and it is a hard abort with hardened libstdc++ -- _GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS, which
Ubuntu's g++ enables by default:

    stl_vector.h:1282: ... Assertion '__n < this->size()' failed.

Reached from FBXConverter::ConvertWeights while converting skin clusters.  Four
of the owner's eight Mixamo FBX exports abort on load in a Debug build
(in_index 14041, offset 54915 == m_mappings.size(), count 0); in an optimised
build the same line is a silent out-of-range vector access.

The fix
-------
Return an empty result -- count 0 and a valid, non-null address -- instead of
indexing the vector.  All three call sites (ConvertWeights and the two
blend-shape loops in FBXConverter.cpp) iterate [0, count) and so do nothing for
an empty span, but ConvertWeights ai_asserts the pointer is non-null, hence the
static sentinel rather than nullptr.  The same path also absorbs a corrupt
adjacency table (offset + count past the end) that the old code only ai_asserted
on, so release builds stop reading out of range there too.

No behavioural change for well-formed vertices: those still get
m_mappings.data() + offset, the same address the old expression produced.

diff --git a/code/AssetLib/FBX/FBXMeshGeometry.cpp b/code/AssetLib/FBX/FBXMeshGeometry.cpp
--- a/code/AssetLib/FBX/FBXMeshGeometry.cpp
+++ b/code/AssetLib/FBX/FBXMeshGeometry.cpp
@@ -230,6 +230,13 @@ const MatIndexArray& MeshGeometry::GetMaterialIndices() const {
 }
 // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 const unsigned int* MeshGeometry::ToOutputVertexIndex( unsigned int in_index, unsigned int& count ) const {
+    // Non-null return + count == 0 means "this input vertex has no output
+    // mapping".  Every caller (ConvertWeights, and both blend-shape loops in
+    // FBXConverter) iterates [0, count) and never dereferences the pointer in
+    // that case, but ConvertWeights does ai_assert the pointer is non-null, so
+    // the empty result must still be a valid address.
+    static const unsigned int no_mapping = 0;
+
     if ( in_index >= m_mapping_counts.size() ) {
         return nullptr;
     }
@@ -237,9 +244,21 @@ const unsigned int* MeshGeometry::ToOutputVertexIndex( unsigned int in_index, un
     ai_assert( m_mapping_counts.size() == m_mapping_offsets.size() );
     count = m_mapping_counts[ in_index ];
 
-    ai_assert( m_mapping_offsets[ in_index ] + count <= m_mappings.size() );
+    const size_t offset = static_cast< size_t >( m_mapping_offsets[ in_index ] );
+    if ( count == 0 || offset + count > m_mappings.size() ) {
+        // A vertex that no polygon references has count == 0 and, when it is at
+        // the tail of the vertex list, an offset equal to m_mappings.size().
+        // &m_mappings[ offset ] then forms a reference to the one-past-the-end
+        // element: undefined behaviour, and a hard abort in hardened libstdc++
+        // builds (_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS, on by default on Ubuntu) -- see assimp
+        // issue #4312.  Mixamo character exports contain such vertices.
+        // A genuinely corrupt adjacency table (offset + count past the end) is
+        // reported the same way instead of handing out an out-of-range span.
+        count = 0;
+        return &no_mapping;
+    }
 
-    return &m_mappings[ m_mapping_offsets[ in_index ] ];
+    return m_mappings.data() + offset;
 }
 
 // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
